    "originalText": "2.4.a Collaborate with Colleagues\nTeaching Channel provides authentic examples of educators collaborating across grade levels and content areas to plan cohesive learning experiences. These videos and Thought Starters give teams a starting point for discussion, shared planning, and designing technology-supported learning that connects classroom instruction to authentic, real-world experiences. \nCollaboration in Action: Educators work together to build a shared unit, align instructional practices, and create consistent, meaningful learning experiences across classrooms.\nLesson Reflection: Planning the Next Step\nELA teacher Erin Gilrein and Global Studies teacher Jen Wolfe reflect on a cross-disciplinary Socratic Seminar. After the lesson, the teachers get together to discuss what went well and what could have used improvement. The teachers plan next steps based on the outcomes of their lessons, reflecting both on what they could do differently as teachers and what students could do differently. They examine the performances of individual students and reflect together on including all students in the discussion.",
    "revisedText": "2.4.a Collaborate with Colleagues\n\nTeaching Channel provides authentic examples of educators collaborating across grade levels and content areas to plan cohesive learning experiences. These videos and Thought Starters give teams a starting point for discussion, shared planning, and designing technology-supported learning that connects classroom instruction to authentic, real-world experiences. \n\nCollaboration in Action: Educators work together to build a shared unit, align instructional practices, and create consistent, meaningful learning experiences across classrooms.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLesson Reflection: Planning the Next Step\nELA teacher Erin Gilrein and Global Studies teacher Jen Wolfe reflect on a cross-disciplinary Socratic Seminar. After the lesson, the teachers get together to discuss what went well and what could have used improvement. The teachers plan next steps based on the outcomes of their lessons, reflecting both on what they could do differently as teachers and what students could do differently. They examine the performances of individual students and reflect together on including all students in the discussion.\n\n\n",
